@group(:concurrency)
mod Std.Otp.Proof
  ## Obligation (1) of the OTP process-algebra metatheory, discharged IN Cure
  ## (spec `docs/research/process-types/2026-07-16-otp-metatheory-in-cure-brief.md`,
  ## findings `docs/research/process-types/2026-07-16-spike-findings-obligation1.md`).
  ##
  ## **The result.** A `call` handler serves a request `req : Req` and answers it
  ## through a *reply capability*. This module establishes, kernel-checked, that:
  ##
  ##   1. the reply value must have the request's OWN reply type `ReplyOf(req)` —
  ##      a *per-constructor* dependent type computed by large elimination; and
  ##   2. the reply capability is *linear* (grade 1): consumed exactly once, on
  ##      every path — never dropped, never duplicated.
  ##
  ## This is **strictly more expressive than the published literature**. NVLang
  ## (2512.05224) types every constructor's reply *uniformly* (one `τr` for the
  ## whole protocol) and has no linearity; Special Delivery (2306.12935) types the
  ## heterogeneous reply but without a linear capability. Cure gets both because it
  ## has large elimination AND native QTT grades in the kernel.
  ##
  ## **What "kernel-checked" buys.** The kernel accepts `reply`, `handle`, and
  ## `serve` below only because each obligation holds. The DUAL — that violations
  ## are *rejected* — is the soundness evidence and lives in the differential
  ## oracle `test/oracle/otp/`, mirrored in Idris 2 (which has the same `1 x : T`
  ## multiplicity and large elimination), where all four probes agree:
  ##   * `ob1_reply_linear`   — accept  / accept   (this module's `serve`)
  ##   * `ob1_neg_wrong_reply`— reject  / reject   (a reply value not `ReplyOf(req)`)
  ##   * `ob1_neg_drop_cap`   — reject  / reject   (capability dropped: `used :erased`)
  ##   * `ob1_neg_dup_cap`    — reject  / reject   (capability duplicated: `used ω`)
  ##
  ## **The example vocabulary** is a three-request server whose replies genuinely
  ## differ per constructor (two distinct reply types, one request carrying a
  ## field) — the smallest shape that exercises heterogeneity. `Req`/`ReplyOf`
  ## stand for any request algebra; the argument is schematic in them.

  ## The request vocabulary. Three constructors; `SetName` carries a field.
  type Req = GetCount | SetName(Reply0) | Ping

  ## The two distinct reply types. `GetCount` answers `Reply0`; `SetName`/`Ping`
  ## answer `Reply1`. (Self-contained ADTs so the argument rests on nothing but the
  ## kernel's large elimination and QTT.)
  type Reply0 = R0
  type Reply1 = R1a | R1b

  ## **Per-constructor reply assignment** — `ReplyOf : Req -> Type` by LARGE
  ## ELIMINATION. This is what makes the reply *heterogeneous*: the type of the
  ## answer is computed from the request constructor. (cf. `Std.Optic`'s
  ## kind-indexed `FocusShape`, oracle cluster `largeelim`.)
  fn ReplyOf(r: Req) -> Type = match r
    GetCount()  -> Reply0
    SetName(_)  -> Reply1
    Ping()      -> Reply1

  ## The **reply capability**, indexed by the request it may answer. A value of
  ## `ReplyCap(r)` is the one-shot right to reply to `r`.
  type ReplyCap(r: Req) indices ()
    MkCap : ReplyCap(r)

  ## The acknowledgement that a reply has been sent.
  type Replied = Done

  ## **The reply rule.** `reply` consumes the capability `cap` LINEARLY (`:linear`,
  ## grade 1) and demands the value `v` at exactly the request's reply type
  ## `ReplyOf(r)`. Matching `cap` consumes it once; there is no path that keeps it.
  ## The kernel's QTT check (`Cure.Elab.Relevance`, the Idris `LinearCheck` dual)
  ## enforces the grade; the dependent domain `ReplyOf(r)` enforces obligation (1.1).
  fn reply({r: Req}, cap :linear ReplyCap(r), v: ReplyOf(r)) -> Replied =
    match cap
      MkCap() -> Done

  ## **The handler's answer**, as a total dependent function: for every request it
  ## produces a value of that request's OWN reply type. `handle` IS the witness of
  ## obligation (1.1) — its type `(r: Req) -> ReplyOf(r)` is checked per branch
  ## against `ReplyOf` reduced at the matched constructor (dependent-return
  ## refinement; the spike1 shape). Totality is kernel-certified, so every request
  ## is answered and answered at the right type.
  fn handle(r: Req) -> ReplyOf(r) = match r
    GetCount()  -> R0
    SetName(_)  -> R1a
    Ping()      -> R1b

  ## **Preservation, composed.** Serving a request is: compute the per-constructor
  ## answer `handle(r) : ReplyOf(r)`, then discharge the linear capability with it
  ## exactly once. `serve` type-checks iff both obligations hold simultaneously —
  ## the reply value preserves the request's reply type AND the capability's
  ## residual multiplicity is 0. Leaving `r` abstract here keeps `ReplyOf(r)`
  ## definitionally equal to `handle`'s result type, so no sibling refinement is
  ## needed; the per-constructor content is carried entirely by `handle`.
  fn serve(r: Req, cap :linear ReplyCap(r)) -> Replied = reply(cap, handle(r))
end
